codex
The patch introduces a Slack authorization bypass for the new busy controls and has functional gaps in Slack progress rendering and the intended default-mode change. These issues should be addressed before considering the change correct.

Full review comments:

- [P1] Enforce runner auth on Slack busy buttons — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/platforms/slack.py:3223-3232
  When a Slack deployment relies on `GATEWAY_ALLOWED_USERS` or pairing with `SLACK_ALLOWED_USERS` unset, this check treats the empty Slack allowlist as allow-all. Block Kit actions bypass the normal message path, so any user who can see the busy buttons can steer, interrupt, or stop another user's active session; route the reconstructed `SessionSource` through `GatewayRunner._is_user_authorized` instead of checking only `SLACK_ALLOWED_USERS`.

- [P2] Preserve Slack busy blocks during progress edits — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/platforms/slack.py:3047-3052
  When buttons are attached to a Slack tool-progress message, this rewrites the message as Block Kit with the visible text captured in the section block. Later progress updates still call `SlackAdapter.edit_message()`, which sends only top-level `text` and never rebuilds these blocks, so Slack clients can keep displaying this stale snapshot while the fallback text changes; update the Slack edit path to refresh the section and actions together while the message is an active busy-button anchor.

- [P2] Update the real busy-input defaults — /Users/wondermonkey/.hermes/hermes-agent-buttons/gateway/run.py:1954-1958
  This queue fallback only applies when no config/env value exists. Normal generated configs still contain `display.busy_input_mode: interrupt` from `hermes_cli/config.py`, and `cli.py` also defaults to `interrupt`; the gateway import bridge then sets `HERMES_GATEWAY_BUSY_INPUT_MODE=interrupt`, so new installs and the CLI remain interrupt-by-default despite the changed example and tests.
The patch introduces a Slack authorization bypass for the new busy controls and has functional gaps in Slack progress rendering and the intended default-mode change. These issues should be addressed before considering the change correct.

Full review comments:

